WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:05.080
Scenario-specific playbooks apply the same structure to different incident types. The

00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:06.900
structure stays familiar:

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:13.600
purpose, scope, triggers, severity, roles, data sources, response actions,

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:19.820
decision points, evidence handling, communications, containment, recovery, closure,

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:22.120
and post-incident tasks.

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:27.460
The content changes because each scenario has different signals, risks, and responders.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:32.160
A phishing playbook may start from a user report or email security alert.

00:00:32.380 --> 00:00:36.700
It should guide responders to preserve message metadata, identify recipients,

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:39.800
check whether links were clicked or credentials were submitted,

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:44.580
quarantine related messages when appropriate, and coordinate user communication.

00:00:44.800 --> 00:00:49.920
A ransomware playbook may focus on affected hosts, business continuity, backups,

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.820
containment, executive escalation, and recovery planning.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:58.680
A malware infection playbook may focus on endpoint telemetry, isolation decisions,

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:01.220
artifact removal, and verification.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:06.880
A lost or stolen device playbook may involve device management, encryption status,

00:01:07.100 --> 00:01:11.980
remote lock or wipe options, user follow-up, and data exposure assessment.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:17.640
Identity-focused scenarios need careful access review. A suspicious login playbook may use

00:01:17.860 --> 00:01:19.380
authentication logs,

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:24.320
device information, multi-factor events, impossible travel patterns,

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.300
and recent account activity.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.640
A privileged account compromise playbook should add stronger escalation, token revocation,

00:01:31.860 --> 00:01:36.740
password reset, role review, audit log review, and approval requirements.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:41.040
A cloud key exposure playbook should identify where the key was found,

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:46.140
what identity it belongs to, what permissions it had, what audit logs show,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:48.680
whether resources were created or changed,

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:52.020
and how rotation or revocation will affect systems.

00:01:52.240 --> 00:01:56.800
Data exfiltration and web application compromise playbooks usually require broad

00:01:57.020 --> 00:01:59.980
coordination. They may involve application logs,

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.320
network telemetry, storage logs, legal and privacy review, customer impact analysis,

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:07.380
and careful communications.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.840
The playbook should stay defensive and evidence-focused. It should help responders

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.500
understand scope,

00:02:13.720 --> 00:02:16.200
protect systems, preserve records,

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:19.540
and coordinate remediation without turning into offensive guidance.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:22.880
Environment-specific details make playbooks useful,

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.900
but too much hard-coded detail makes them hard to maintain.

00:02:26.120 --> 00:02:32.120
Use stable references where possible: group names, evidence locations, ticket templates,

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:36.340
detection names, dashboard links, and role-based contacts.

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:41.760
Avoid embedding personal contact details or tool steps that change every month unless the

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:44.700
organization has a process to keep them current.

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.160
If a technical action is complex,

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:52.340
link to a maintained runbook rather than copying stale instructions into every playbook.

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:57.520
Playbooks should connect to operations. Link them to detection logic, response tools,

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:03.740
ticket templates, evidence repositories, communication templates, and tabletop exercises.

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:07.480
Test them with realistic scenarios. After real incidents,

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:09.620
update them with lessons learned.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:15.760
A playbook that is never tested is only a guess. A playbook that is owned, exercised,

00:03:15.980 --> 00:03:19.340
and improved becomes part of the team's response muscle memory.
